WebCore: the curl WebSocket scheduler waits with poll(), and waits every pass (issue #867).

CurlStreamScheduler::workerThread put every WebSocket's socket in three stack fd_sets and select()ed with
one timeval it reused: select() writes the time left back, so once a wait had run out every later one
returned at once, and the thread spun for as long as a WebSocket was open. A stream whose socket was gone
(it failed or the server closed it; the stream stays listed until the page destroys it) was not waited on
at all: with no socket the loop never slept. A socket of descriptor 1024 or more overflowed the sets.

Now each pass polls the streams' sockets (POLLIN and POLLPRI, POLLOUT while data waits) for up to the same
20 ms, with or without a socket, and hands each stream what poll() reported for its own socket: input, a
hangup, an error or a stale descriptor go to the receive (which reports the end or the failure), output to
the send. curl itself already uses poll() (HAVE_POLL, lib/select.c).

